          "ruleId": "CWE-444",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "HTTP request smuggling (CWE-444) in Message.set_body_reader: the header loop accepts Transfer-Encoding and Content-Length simultaneously without rejecting the conflict, and silently IGNORES every Transfer-Encoding value that is not exactly \"chunked\". Line 134-136 is `elif name == \"TRANSFER-ENCODING\": if value.lower() == \"chunked\": chunked = True` with NO else-branch that raises. Then line 139 `if chunked:` -> ChunkedReader, line 140 `elif content_length is not None:` -> LengthReader. Two concrete desyncs: (a) CL.TE -- a request with both `Content-Length: 6` and `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` is accepted; gunicorn frames the body with ChunkedReader while a front-end proxy honoring Content-Length frames it differently, so trailing bytes become a smuggled pipelined request; (b) TE obfuscation -- `Transfer-Encoding: chunked, identity` / `xchunked` / `chunked ` fails the exact `== \"chunked\"` test, so gunicorn ignores TE entirely and falls back to Content-Length, while a proxy that substring-matches or last-token-matches \"chunked\" uses chunked framing. Request.set_body_reader (line 357) overrides this but adds ONLY an EOFReader->LengthReader(0) substitution (line 359-360), so it introduces no TE/CL conflict validation.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. gunicorn/http/message.py:125-154 (Message.set_body_reader) performs body framing with two confirmed RFC 7230 \u00a73.3.3 violations. (a) The header loop (129-136) collects Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding independently and NEVER rejects their co-presence; line 138 `if chunked:` unconditionally wins, so `Content-Length: 6` + `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` is accepted and the CL value is silently dropped without even reaching the numeric/negative validation at 142-150 (it lives in the unreached `elif`). A front-end honoring CL and gunicorn honoring TE desync, and the residual bytes are parsed as a pipelined request by RequestParser.__next__ (parser.py:42) on the keep-alive connection. (b) Line 135 is an exact `value.lower() == \"chunked\"` match with no else-branch raise, so `chunked, identity`, `identity, chunked`, `xchunked`, `chunked;q=1` leave chunked=False and gunicorn falls back to LengthReader while a proxy that substring/last-token matches \"chunked\" uses chunked framing \u2014 the reverse desync. I confirmed no compensating validation exists anywhere: a repo-wide grep for request-side Transfer-Encoding handling yields exactly one hit, message.py:134 (wsgi.py:312 is the response path, util.py:46 is the hop-by-hop name list). Request.set_body_reader (357-360) only swaps EOFReader for LengthReader(0) and adds no conflict check. Reachability is genuine, not just a graph label: set_body_reader() is invoked unconditionally from Message.__init__ (message.py:57) for every request a worker parses; the graph concurs (all 4 set_body_reader nodes reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0). Version pinned to gunicorn 21.2.0 (gunicorn/__init__.py:6; news.rst top entry 21.2.0 - 2023-07-19, release HEAD, no backport), which predates the upstream TE-validation fix in 22.0.0 (CVE-2024-1135) \u2014 advisory only, since exploit_search returned \"corpus unavailable: not indexed\" and I could not verify an affected range from a module. One analyst sub-claim is wrong and I discard it: the `\"chunked \"` trailing-space bypass does NOT work, because parse_headers applies `value = ''.join(value).rstrip()` at message.py:106. The analyst's line range 125-152 is also short by two lines; the function ends at 154. The core finding stands on the remaining vectors."
          },
