        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-807",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Client-controlled SCRIPT_NAME request header is trusted into the WSGI environ, and also yields an unhandled IndexError remote DoS. In wsgi.create() the loop over req.headers at line 127-128 does `elif hdr_name == \"SCRIPT_NAME\": script_name = hdr_value` -- hdr_name is the request header name uppercased by message.py:92 (`name = name.upper()`), so any client sending a header literally named `Script_Name:` / `SCRIPT_NAME:` sets it. HEADER_RE (message.py:24) is a blocklist `[\\x00-\\x1F\\x7F()<>@,;:\\[\\]={} \\t\\\\\\\"]` that does NOT contain underscore, so the underscore name passes header-name validation. The value then reaches `environ['SCRIPT_NAME'] = script_name` (line 185), controlling the WSGI app's mount-point/routing and URL generation. Critically, line 183 computes `path_info = path_info.split(script_name, 1)[1]`: if script_name is not a substring of req.path, split returns a 1-element list and the `[1]` index raises IndexError, an unhandled exception in request setup -- e.g. `GET / HTTP/1.1` with `Script_Name: /nomatch` gives `\"/\".split(\"/nomatch\",1)` == `[\"/\"]` -> IndexError. I verified there is NO trust gating on this path: a query for any forwarded_allow_ips / peer_addr / secure_scheme reference anywhere in gunicorn/http/wsgi.py returned 0 rows, so unlike the scheme headers (gated in message.py:70-75) and PROXY protocol (gated by proxy_allow_ips in message.py:280-283), SCRIPT_NAME is accepted from any peer.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Core claim re-derived from source and confirmed; one sub-claim corrected. CONFIRMED: an unauthenticated client can set the WSGI mount point. wsgi.py:127-128 does `elif hdr_name == \"SCRIPT_NAME\": script_name = hdr_value`; header names are only uppercased (message.py:92) with no hyphen->underscore normalization, so a header literally named `Script_Name:` produces `SCRIPT_NAME` and matches, and HEADER_RE (message.py:24) is a blocklist that omits underscore (\\x5F) so the name passes validation at message.py:93-94. The value reaches `environ['SCRIPT_NAME'] = script_name` (wsgi.py:185) and truncates PATH_INFO via `path_info.split(script_name, 1)[1]` (wsgi.py:183) where path_info is the client URI path (message.py:347). I independently verified there is NO trust gating: my own grep for `forwarded_allow_ips` across gunicorn/ returns hits only in config.py:1259 and message.py:72-74 and ZERO in wsgi.py, so unlike the scheme headers (peer-gated at message.py:72-75) SCRIPT_NAME is honored from any peer. Graph confirms reachability: the line 128/183/185 calls are reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1. Impact: `GET /public/admin` with `Script_Name: /public` yields SCRIPT_NAME=/public and PATH_INFO=/admin, so the app sees a path the fronting proxy never authorized (path-ACL bypass), and attacker-controlled SCRIPT_NAME poisons framework URL generation (Flask url_for / Django reverse) enabling open redirect and cache poisoning. CWE-807 (reliance on untrusted input in a security decision) is the tighter fit, within the analyst's CWE-20 class. CORRECTED: the analyst's \"unhandled IndexError remote DoS\" is FALSE. The IndexError at wsgi.py:183 is real (e.g. \"/\".split(\"/nomatch\",1) == [\"/\"], so [1] raises), but it is caught by every worker -- sync.py:157-158, gthread.py:308-309, base_async.py:85-86 all `except Exception` -> handle_error, and base.py:251-271 writes a 500 and returns without re-raising. The worker stays alive, so this is a per-request 500, not a crash or DoS. Severity therefore rests on the mount-point/PATH_INFO control, not availability. Mitigating factors keep this at MEDIUM rather than HIGH: the behavior is documented as intended at docs/source/faq.rst:13-14 (\"The value could be set by setting SCRIPT_NAME in the environment or as an HTTP header\"), nginx drops underscore headers by default (underscores_in_headers off) so it chiefly affects directly-exposed gunicorn or proxies that forward underscore names, and the concrete consequence depends on the hosted app. Version pinned to gunicorn 21.2.0 (gunicorn/__init__.py:6, newest news.rst entry at line 5); exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so there is no exploit-reference signal confirming or patching this -- the verdict rests entirely on source evidence I gathered."
          },
