        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-444",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Proxy-header spoofing via underscore/dash collision when building the WSGI environ (CWE-444 inconsistent interpretation). wsgi.create() line 136 computes `key = 'HTTP_' + hdr_name.replace('-', '_')`, and lines 137-139 MERGE a repeated key by concatenation: `if key in environ: hdr_value = \"%s,%s\" % (environ[key], hdr_value)` then `environ[key] = hdr_value`. Because HEADER_RE (message.py:24) does not blocklist underscore, a request header named `X_Forwarded_For` is a valid header name and maps to the SAME environ key `HTTP_X_FORWARDED_FOR` as the dashed `X-Forwarded-For`. A front-end proxy that strips/overwrites only the dashed spelling will not touch the underscored one, so the attacker's value is comma-merged into the trusted header the application reads for client-IP/ACL decisions -- letting the attacker prepend or append arbitrary entries to X-Forwarded-For (and equivalently to any other HTTP_* header the app trusts). No deduplication or underscore rejection exists on this path.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. gunicorn/http/message.py:24 defines HEADER_RE as a blocklist of control chars, DEL and HTTP separators; underscore (\\x5F) is absent, and message.py:93-94 is the ONLY header-name validation on the parse path, so a header literally named `X_Forwarded_For` is accepted and uppercased (message.py:90/92) to `X_FORWARDED_FOR`. gunicorn/http/wsgi.py:136 then computes `key = 'HTTP_' + hdr_name.replace('-', '_')`, which maps BOTH `X_FORWARDED_FOR` and `X-FORWARDED-FOR` to the identical environ key `HTTP_X_FORWARDED_FOR`, and lines 137-139 comma-MERGE the collision (`hdr_value = \"%s,%s\" % (environ[key], hdr_value)`) instead of rejecting or deduplicating it. default_environ/base_environ (wsgi.py:68-91) seed no HTTP_* keys, so the merge is purely between the proxy-supplied dashed header and the attacker-supplied underscored one. A front-end proxy that strips or overwrites only the dashed spelling leaves the underscored spelling intact, so the attacker injects an arbitrary entry into the comma-list the application parses -- client-IP spoofing for ACLs, rate limits and logging (and the same trick applies to any other HTTP_* header the app trusts). Reachability is established independently of the graph: wsgi.create() is called on the primary request path of every worker (workers/sync.py:168, workers/gthread.py:319, workers/base_async.py:96); the graph agrees (all calls at wsgi.py:136-139 have reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1). No mitigation exists in this tree: a case-insensitive grep for `underscore|header_map|header-map` across the entire repo returns zero matches, i.e. the later --header-map (drop/refuse/dangerous) hardening is absent from this build, which is pinned at gunicorn 21.2.0 (gunicorn/__init__.py:6). Note gunicorn's own secure_scheme_headers lookup (message.py:111) uses the raw dashed name, so gunicorn's internal scheme decision is not directly flipped -- the impact lands on the WSGI application downstream, which is why severity is MEDIUM rather than HIGH and why exploitability is conditioned on a proxy-fronted deployment whose app trusts a forwarded header. Exploit-reference check could not be performed: exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no published module/CVE/version-range comparison backs or refutes this; the verdict rests entirely on the source and graph evidence above."
          },
