        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-20",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Request method is effectively unvalidated past its first few characters, flowing to environ['REQUEST_METHOD'] and the access log (CWE-20). message.py:25 defines `METH_RE = re.compile(r\"[A-Z0-9$-_.]{3,20}\")` and line 336 tests it with `if not METH_RE.match(bits[0])`. Two compounding defects: (1) the pattern has NO end anchor (`$`/`\\Z`) and `re.match` anchors only at the start, so once the first 3 characters match the class the ENTIRE remainder of the token is unchecked and arbitrary -- `bits[0]` is only bounded by the whitespace split at line 331 (`line_bytes.split(None, 2)`); (2) the character class contains `$-_`, which Python parses as a RANGE from \\x24 to \\x5F, silently admitting `%&'()*+,-./0123456789:;<=>?@`, `[`, `\\`, `]` and `^` rather than the intended literal `$`, `-`, `_`. The value is then stored at line 338 (`self.method = bits[0].upper()`) and propagated to `environ[\"REQUEST_METHOD\"] = req.method` (wsgi.py:83/86) and into the access-log atoms 'm' and 'r' (glogging.py:297-303), so a downstream app, proxy or log consumer that treats the method differently from gunicorn can be desynchronized or injected into.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source. Both defects the lead describes are real and present. (1) message.py:25 defines METH_RE = re.compile(r\"[A-Z0-9$-_.]{3,20}\") with no end anchor, and message.py:336 validates with METH_RE.match(bits[0]) -- I grepped the entire package and lines 25/336 are the ONLY uses of METH_RE, with no fullmatch/TOKEN_RE/METHOD_BADCHAR_RE anywhere, so once the first 3 chars fall in the class the whole remainder of the token is unchecked and the {3,20} length cap is silently void (bounded only by limit_request_line, default 4094, via read_line at message.py:239-250). (2) Inside the class, `$`(\\x24) and `_`(\\x5F) are bare literals flanking `-`, so Python compiles a RANGE \\x24-\\x5F that subsumes A-Z/0-9 and additionally admits %&'()*+,-./:;<=>?@[\\]^ -- a 3-char method of `[\\]` or `@@@` is accepted. Because message.py:331 splits on whitespace and bytes_to_str is str(b,'latin1') (util.py:647-650), CR/LF/space cannot appear, but NUL (\\x00), ESC (\\x1b), DEL and 0x80-0xFF all can in the unchecked tail. The value is stored at message.py:338 and propagates to environ[\"REQUEST_METHOD\"] (wsgi.py:86 -- correcting the lead's \"83\", which is the env.update({ line) and into access-log atoms 'r' (glogging.py:302-304) and 'm' (glogging.py:306), emitted unsanitized at glogging.py:363 with no escaping of atom values. Reachability is established both ways: the graph marks Request.parse_request_line (line 330) as an EntryPoint handler with exposure='reachable' and the calls at 331/336/338 as reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1; the source chain is Parser.__next__ -> Message.__init__:55 -> Request.parse:208 -> parse_request_line. Author intent is proven by the project's own test tests/requests/invalid/003.http (\"-blargh /foo HTTP/1.1\" expected to raise InvalidRequestMethod) which passes only by accident -- \"-BLargh\" or \"GETfoo\" would be accepted. I calibrate severity to MEDIUM rather than HIGH: no CR/LF/space can reach bits[0], so this is not self-contained request smuggling; the demonstrated impact is RFC 9110 token-validation bypass creating a gunicorn-vs-intermediary parser discrepancy plus control-char/ANSI-escape injection into the access log. Version pinned to gunicorn 21.2.0 (gunicorn/__init__.py:6); exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no exploit-reference grounding was used."
          },
