          "ruleId": "CWE-113",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Response status line is written without the CRLF/control-character validation that gunicorn applies to every other response header (CWE-113 response splitting). Response.start_response stores the app-supplied status verbatim at wsgi.py:233 (`self.status = status`); the only subsequent operation is line 239 `self.status_code = int(self.status.split()[0])`, whose failure is swallowed into `self.status_code = None` at line 241. default_headers then interpolates it straight into the status line at line 304-306: `\"HTTP/%s.%s %s\\r\\n\" % (self.req.version[0], self.req.version[1], self.status)`, and send_headers (line 318-319) emits it. By contrast process_headers validates header NAMES with HEADER_RE (line 252-253) and header VALUES with `HEADER_VALUE_RE = re.compile(r'[\\x00-\\x1F\\x7F]')` (wsgi.py:21, checked at line 258-259) -- status bypasses both. A WSGI application that places request-derived text into its status reason-phrase (a pattern that is safe on servers which validate status) therefore permits header/body injection into the response. This is second-order: it needs the hosted app to pass attacker-influenced status, so it is a missing-defense gap rather than a directly reachable flow -- ranked lowest for that reason.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source: Response.start_response stores the app-supplied status string verbatim (gunicorn/http/wsgi.py:233) with no type/CRLF/control-character validation; the only use before dispatch is `int(self.status.split()[0])` (239), whose failure is swallowed to None (241) and which yields \"200\" for \"200 OK\\r\\nX: y\", so it provides no incidental protection. The value is then interpolated directly into the status line `\"HTTP/%s.%s %s\\r\\n\" % (..., self.status)` (304-306) and written to the socket latin-1 encoded (315-322), which passes \\r and \\n through unchanged. By contrast the sibling input is validated: process_headers rejects header names via HEADER_RE (252-253) and header values via HEADER_VALUE_RE = re.compile(r'[\\x00-\\x1F\\x7F]') (wsgi.py:21, checked 258-259). The asymmetry the lead describes is real and present. Reachability is real, not just graph-asserted: every worker hands this exact callable to the application (workers/sync.py:178, workers/gthread.py:334, workers/base_async.py:108 `respiter = self.wsgi(environ, resp.start_response)`), and the graph marks start_response/process_headers/default_headers/send_headers all reachable_from_entry=true. I confirmed there is no first-order injection inside gunicorn itself: the only in-tree writer of resp.status (workers/base.py:264) builds it from hard-coded reason literals and that Response is used only for the access log, while the real error bytes go through util.write_error (util.py:314-334). So this is a genuine missing-defense gap (CWE-113) whose exploitation requires the hosted app to place attacker-influenced text in the status reason-phrase (a pattern frameworks permit, e.g. an unvalidated Django reason_phrase) - hence LOW severity rather than a directly exploitable flow. Exploit-reference grounding was not possible: the Metasploit corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", and I could not establish whether upstream later added status validation, so I neither claim a weaponized exploit nor claim this is patched here; version is pinned exactly at 21.2.0 (gunicorn/__init__.py:6)."
