          "ruleId": "CWE-444",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Message.set_body_reader (gunicorn/http/message.py:125) recognizes a Transfer-Encoding header ONLY when the value is exactly \"chunked\" (`if value.lower() == \"chunked\": chunked = True`, lines 134-136). There is NO `else: raise InvalidHeader(\"TRANSFER-ENCODING\")` branch \u2014 any other TE value (`identity`, `chunked, chunked`, `xchunked`, `chunked;a=b`, ` chunked`) is silently DISCARDED and the request then falls through to the Content-Length branch at line 140. RFC 7230 requires an origin server to reject a request with an unrecognized Transfer-Encoding with 501/400. Missing control => HTTP request smuggling (TE.CL desync) whenever a front-end proxy honors a TE form gunicorn ignores.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from the real source. gunicorn/http/message.py:134-136 recognizes Transfer-Encoding only when the full value equals \"chunked\" and has NO else-branch rejecting unrecognized values, so any other TE form (identity, \"chunked, chunked\", xchunked, \"chunked;a=b\") is silently discarded and control falls through to the Content-Length branch at line 140, framing the body with LengthReader. All four raises in set_body_reader (132, 145, 147, 150) are InvalidHeader(\"CONTENT-LENGTH\"); none covers TRANSFER-ENCODING. A repo-wide grep shows message.py:134-136 is the ONLY request-side TE handling in the package (util.py:46 is just a hop-by-hop ignore list, wsgi.py is response-side), and the Request.set_body_reader override at 357-360 only swaps EOFReader->LengthReader(0), adding no validation. Reachability is genuine rather than an arrow-function artifact: set_body_reader() is invoked unconditionally from Message.__init__ (line 57) for every parsed request, and the graph reports Message.set_body_reader reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0 with all 46 call nodes in lines 129-152 reachable_from_entry=true. The graph independently confirms the gap: between the chunked=True assignment (136) and Body(ChunkedReader(...)) (139) there are zero nodes - no <operator>.raise and no InvalidHeader. Desync is completable because parser.py:42 reuses the same unreader across keep-alive requests, so bytes past Content-Length are parsed as the next request line (TE.CL smuggling whenever a front-end honors a TE form gunicorn drops). RFC 7230 requires an origin server to answer an unrecognized Transfer-Encoding with 501/400. Version pinned at 21.2.0 (gunicorn/__init__.py:6), which predates the 22.0.0 strict-TE hardening (CVE-2024-1135); note that exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so this version conclusion rests on the source/version boundary, not a catalog hit. One correction to the analyst: its example \" chunked\" (leading space) IS actually accepted, because parse_headers lstrips (line 96) and rstrips (line 106) the value - this does not affect the core claim."
