          "ruleId": "CWE-113",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Request header VALUES are never validated for control characters. In gunicorn/http/message.py the only regex validation applied during request parsing is `HEADER_RE.search(name)` at line 93 \u2014 the header NAME. A `HEADER_VALUE_RE` control does exist in this codebase but ONLY on the response path (gunicorn/http/wsgi.py:21 `re.compile(r'[\\x00-\\x1F\\x7F]')`, applied at wsgi.py:258); there is no counterpart on the request path. Because parse_headers splits exclusively on CRLF (line 67 `data.split(b\"\\r\\n\")`) and request framing uses `b\"\\r\\n\\r\\n\"` (lines 214/218), a bare LF, bare CR, NUL or vertical-tab embedded in a header value survives verbatim into the header list and the WSGI environ. Missing control => request smuggling against any peer that splits headers on bare LF (e.g. `X-Foo: a\\nTransfer-Encoding: chunked` is one header to gunicorn, two to a lenient proxy), plus access-log injection since these same values are fed to the access log at glogging.py:330.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source: gunicorn/http/message.py parse_headers validates ONLY the header name (line 93 `HEADER_RE.search(name)`); the value is split off at line 88, lstrip/rstrip'd at 96/106 and appended at line 121 with no character validation. A repo-wide grep shows the only control-char value filter, HEADER_VALUE_RE, exists solely on the RESPONSE path (wsgi.py:21 def, wsgi.py:258 applied in Response.process_headers) with no request-path counterpart. Framing is CRLF-exclusive (line 67 `data.split(b\"\\r\\n\")`, lines 214/218 `data.find(b\"\\r\\n\\r\\n\")`) and util.bytes_to_str (util.py:647-650) decodes latin1, so a bare LF/CR/NUL/VT embedded in a value is byte-preserved into a single (name, value) tuple. Concretely `X-Foo: a\\nTransfer-Encoding: chunked\\r\\n\\r\\n` yields name \"X-FOO\" (passes HEADER_RE) and value \"a\\nTransfer-Encoding: chunked\" \u2014 one header to gunicorn, two to any peer that terminates lines on bare LF. The one-sidedness is sharpened by set_body_reader (lines 129-137), which honors TE only on an exact `value.lower() == \"chunked\"`, so a smuggled `Transfer-Encoding: chunked\\nX-Pad: 1` is silently ignored by gunicorn while visible to the lenient peer (CL is incidentally guarded by isnumeric() at line 142; TE is not). The tainted values reach real sinks: WSGI environ at wsgi.py:120-139 and access-log atoms at glogging.py:330, emitted unescaped through access_log.info at glogging.py:363 (log injection). Graph confirms reachability: Message.parse_headers, wsgi.create and Logger.atoms all have reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0, and the only regex calls in message.py are lines 93/336/352 (name, method, version) \u2014 never `value`. Same parse_headers is reused for chunked trailers (body.py:54). Version is pinned at 21.2.0 (gunicorn/__init__.py:6), predating the 22.0.0 request-path header-value hardening; the Metasploit corpus is NOT indexed in this environment (exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable\"), so no module/CVE-backed exploitability claim is made and the verdict rests only on the source evidence. Severity HIGH rather than CRITICAL because the smuggling impact is deployment-dependent (needs a peer that splits on bare LF), though the access-log injection is unconditional."
