          "ruleId": "CWE-444",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "parse_headers accepts obs-fold / header line folding: lines 98-105 implement `while lines and lines[0].startswith((\" \", \"\\t\")): curr = lines.pop(0); ...; value.append(curr)`, joining continuation lines into the value. RFC 7230 \u00a73.2.4 requires an origin server to reject (or replace with SP) obs-fold in a request. The missing rejection interacts with the exact-match Transfer-Encoding test at line 135: a folded `Transfer-Encoding:\\r\\n\\tchunked` yields the joined value `\"\\tchunked\"` (continuation whitespace is preserved \u2014 only the FIRST segment is lstripped at line 96 and the joined result is only rstripped at line 106), so `value.lower() == \"chunked\"` is False and gunicorn frames the request by Content-Length while a proxy that unfolds correctly sees chunked => TE.CL desync.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from the real source. gunicorn/http/message.py:98-105 implements HTTP header line folding (obs-fold) by popping continuation lines and appending them to the value list, with no rejection and no replacement of the fold with SP as RFC 7230  3.2.4 requires of an origin server; the only raise in that branch is the unrelated LimitRequestHeaders size check (L102-104). The whitespace claim is exact: only the first segment is lstripped (L96, `[value.lstrip()]`) and only the joined result is rstripped (L106, `''.join(value).rstrip()`), so interior continuation whitespace survives. I traced `Transfer-Encoding:\\r\\n\\tchunked`: L67 split yields lines [..., \"Transfer-Encoding:\\r\\n\", \"\\tchunked\\r\\n\"]; L96 gives value=[\"\"] (since \"\\r\\n\".lstrip()==\"\"); L99 \"\\tchunked\\r\\n\".startswith(\"\\t\") is True so L105 appends it; L106 produces the value \"\\tchunked\". At the exact-match sink L135, \"\\tchunked\".lower() != \"chunked\", so chunked stays False and gunicorn frames the body by Content-Length (L140-152, LengthReader), while a proxy that correctly unfolds obs-fold sees Transfer-Encoding: chunked and