          "ruleId": "CWE-770",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "No bound is enforced on the buffer accumulated while scanning for a chunk-size line or for chunked trailers. ChunkedReader.parse_chunk_size (gunicorn/http/body.py:75) loops `while idx < 0: self.get_data(unreader, buf)` appending socket data into an unbounded io.BytesIO until a CRLF appears, and parse_trailers (body.py:41) does the same until `\\r\\n\\r\\n`. The configured `limit_request_line` / `limit_request_field_size` / `max_buffer_headers` guards that protect the request-line and header block (message.py:199, 224, 102/108) are never consulted in body.py. Missing control => a client sending `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` followed by an endless stream containing no CRLF drives unbounded per-connection memory growth (memory-exhaustion DoS).\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. ChunkedReader.parse_chunk_size (body.py:80-83) runs `while idx < 0: self.get_data(unreader, buf)` with no size test, and get_data (body.py:102-106) appends up to 8192 bytes per iteration from sock.recv (unreader.py:63-64), raising NoMoreData only at EOF -- so a client streaming bytes that contain no CRLF grows the per-connection io.BytesIO without bound. parse_trailers (body.py:47-50) has the identical unbounded loop scanning for \\r\\n\\r\\n. The claimed asymmetry is exact: every LimitRequest* enforcement site in the package is in message.py (lines 81, 103, 109, 224-225, 247, 250) and NONE is in body.py; config.py defines only limit_request_line/limit_request_fields/limit_request_field_size, so no limit_request_body knob exists for an operator to mitigate with. Reachability is confirmed in source, not just the graph: message.py:138-139 builds Body(ChunkedReader(...)) on `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`, wsgi.py:84 exposes it as environ['wsgi.input'], and an app read drives Body.read (215) -> ChunkedReader.read (28-30) -> next(parser) -> parse_chunked (58) -> parse_chunk_size; the graph independently marks parse_chunk_size/parse_trailers/get_data reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0. Two aggravating facts the analyst missed: (1) gevent/eventlet workers call notify() from an independent 1s greenlet loop (ggevent.py:86-88, geventlet.py:171-173), so murder_workers (arbiter.py:489-498) never reaps a handler stuck in the loop -- no time bound there, and worker_connections (default 1000) multiplies it per process; the default sync worker IS reaped after `timeout` (~30s), which caps one connection but still permits a multi-GB spike plus worker churn. (2) Line 83 re-runs buf.getvalue().find() each iteration, copying the whole buffer, giving O(n^2) memcpy so CPU exhaustion arrives before memory exhaustion. Severity HIGH rather than CRITICAL: remote, unauthenticated, no config mitigation, but availability-only impact with partial sync-worker reaping. Note: the exploit-reference check could not be performed -- the Metasploit corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\" -- so despite pinning the exact version (21.2.0, gunicorn/__init__.py:6) I assert neither patched nor catalog-exploitable; this verdict rests solely on the source evidence."
