          "ruleId": "CWE-444",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Header names containing underscores are not rejected, and the environ builder conflates underscore with dash, so a proxy's header filtering can be bypassed. HEADER_RE (gunicorn/http/message.py:24) blacklists `[\\x00-\\x1F\\x7F()<>@,;:\\[\\]={} \\t\\\\\"]` \u2014 `_` is NOT in that set, so `X_Forwarded_For` passes validation at line 93. Response/environ construction at gunicorn/http/wsgi.py:136 then computes `key = 'HTTP_' + hdr_name.replace('-', '_')`, mapping both `X-Forwarded-For` and `X_Forwarded_For` onto the same `HTTP_X_FORWARDED_FOR` key, and lines 137-139 merge duplicates with a comma. The absent control is the equivalent of nginx's `underscores_in_headers off` (drop underscore headers). Missing control => an attacker injects `X_Forwarded_For: <spoofed>` which a front-end that only strips the dash form forwards untouched, letting the WSGI app read a spoofed client IP / trust marker.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source. (1) gunicorn/http/message.py:24 defines HEADER_RE = re.compile(r\"[\\x00-\\x1F\\x7F()<>@,;:\\[\\]={} \\t\\\\\\\"]\") \u2014 underscore is NOT in the character class, and line 93-94 (`if HEADER_RE.search(name): raise InvalidHeaderName(name)`) is the only header-name validation, so `X_Forwarded_For` is accepted. (2) gunicorn/http/wsgi.py:136 computes `key = 'HTTP_' + hdr_name.replace('-', '_')`, collapsing `X-Forwarded-For` and `X_Forwarded_For` onto the identical WSGI key `HTTP_X_FORWARDED_FOR`; lines 137-139 then comma-merge the duplicate, splicing the attacker-controlled value into the same list as the proxy-supplied trusted value. Apps using the standard leftmost-is-original-client convention read the spoofed entry. (3) Reachability is real, not an arrow-function graph artifact: graph reports reachable_from_entry=true / hop_distance=1 for all of lines 136-139, and in source `wsgi.create()` is called per-request from workers/sync.py:168, workers/gthread.py:319 and workers/base_async.py:96. (4) The claimed missing control is genuinely missing: a case-insensitive tree-wide grep for `underscore|header_map` yields zero hits under gunicorn/ \u2014 there is no equivalent of nginx `underscores_in_headers off` / the later `header_map` drop|refuse setting. Version pinned to 21.2.0 at gunicorn/__init__.py:6 (`version_info = (21, 2, 0)`), which predates that control. Scope correction limiting severity to MEDIUM: secure_scheme_headers matching at message.py:111 compares dash-form names (config.py:1231-1233), so the underscore variant cannot directly flip req.scheme; impact is confined to application-level trust of HTTP_X_FORWARDED_* and other HTTP_* trust markers, and requires a front-end that forwards underscore headers (nginx drops them by default, Apache/HAProxy/many ALBs do not). Exploit-reference grounding was attempted but mcp__orion__exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no exploit evidence was used either way. CWE corrected from the analyst's CWE-290 to CWE-444, since the root cause is a proxy/server inconsistent-interpretation mismatch (header-filter bypass); IP/trust-marker spoofing (CWE-290) is the downstream effect."
