        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-20",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "METH_RE = re.compile(r\"[A-Z0-9$-_.]{3,20}\") in gunicorn/http/message.py:25 has TWO compounding defects in the literal/usage pair, and it is the only validation applied to the attacker-supplied HTTP request method. (1) `$-_` inside the character class is parsed by Python's re as a RANGE 0x24-0x5F, not three literal chars, so the class silently admits `% & ' ( ) * + , - . / 0-9 : ; < = > ? @ A-Z [ \\ ] ^ _` -- e.g. a method of `@@@` or `///` or `<<<` passes. (2) It is applied with `.match()` (line 336), which anchors only at the START and has no trailing `$`/`fullmatch`, so the `{3,20}` upper bound is never enforced and arbitrary trailing bytes are accepted: `METH_RE.match(\"GET\" + <anything>)` succeeds on the 3-char prefix. Because bits[0] comes from `bytes_to_str` = `str(b,'latin1')` (gunicorn/util.py:650), every raw byte 0x00-0xFF reaches this check 1:1. The accepted token is then stored verbatim as `self.method = bits[0].upper()` (line 338) and forwarded as REQUEST_METHOD, so a reverse proxy and gunicorn can disagree on where the method token ends -> request smuggling / method confusion. parse_request_line is an :EntryPoint handler (hop_distance 0).\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Both claimed defects in the literal/usage pair are real and I re-derived each from the source. (1) In `[A-Z0-9$-_.]` the `$-_` sequence is parsed by Python `re` as a character RANGE 0x24-0x5F (the `-` is between two literals, not escaped, not class-final; Python errors only on reversed ranges, and 0x24<=0x5F so it compiles silently). The effective class collapses to exactly 0x24-0x5F = `$ % & ' ( ) * + , - . / 0-9 : ; < = > ? @ A-Z [ \\ ] ^ _`, which admits 14 RFC 9110 S5.6.2 delimiters forbidden in a method token, so `@@@`, `///`, `<<<`, `:::` all validate, while legitimate tchars (`! # ' * ^ ` | ~`) and lowercase are rejected. (2) It is applied with `.match()` (line 336), which anchors only at position 0 and never requires consuming the whole string, so the `{3,20}` upper bound is unenforceable and arbitrary trailing bytes pass: for `\"GET\"+\"a\"*5000` the quantifier stops at 3 reps (satisfying {3,20}) and returns a truthy match; `\"A\"*50` matches its first 20. Grep over the whole tree shows METH_RE occurs exactly twice and there is no `fullmatch`, `TOKEN_RE`, or `METHOD_BADCHAR_RE` anywhere, confirming this is the only method validation and is the pre-hardening form (version pinned 21.2.0). `bytes_to_str` = `str(b,'latin1')` maps 0x00-0xFF 1:1, so raw bytes reach the check unnormalized; the token is stored verbatim at line 338 and forwarded as REQUEST_METHOD (wsgi.py:86) unsanitized. Reachability is genuine rather than a graph artifact: parse() reads the raw request line from the socket and calls parse_request_line(line) at message.py:208, and my own Cypher query returns exposure=reachable, kind=handler, hop_distance=0, reachable_from_entry=true for the line-336 call. Security impact is concrete because `self.req.method == 'HEAD'` exact-string tests at wsgi.py:217/286 govern keep-alive and chunked framing, so a token a front-end normalizes to HEAD but gunicorn retains as `HEAD<junk>` desynchronizes response framing. Severity held at MEDIUM rather than HIGH: the validation flaw itself is certain and pre-auth, but full smuggling requires a cooperating front-end that disagrees on the token boundary, which I did not verify is deployed, and `split(None,2)` strips CR/LF so no log/header injection is available. exploit_search returned \"corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no exploit-reference evidence was obtained in either direction and none is relied upon."
          },
