        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-444",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "VERSION_RE = re.compile(r\"HTTP/(\\d+)\\.(\\d+)\") in gunicorn/http/message.py:26 is unanchored at BOTH ends and is applied with `.match()` at line 352 to the attacker-controlled HTTP-version token, so trailing garbage is silently accepted and discarded. Critically, the request line is tokenized as `line_bytes.split(None, 2)` (line 331) with maxsplit=2, so bits[2] is the entire remainder of the line and MAY CONTAIN SPACES. A request line `GET / HTTP/1.1 <arbitrary text>` yields bits[2]=\"HTTP/1.1 <arbitrary text>\", `VERSION_RE.match` matches the \"HTTP/1.1\" prefix, `match is None` is False (line 353), and `self.version = (int(match.group(1)), int(match.group(2)))` = (1,1) -- the trailing text is dropped with no error. There is no `$` / `fullmatch` and no length bound on `\\d+`, so `HTTP/0000000001.1` is also accepted. A front-end proxy that rejects or re-interprets that trailing text while gunicorn accepts it produces request-line desync / smuggling. parse_request_line is an :EntryPoint handler (hop_distance 0).\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source, every structural element of the lead is present and correct. gunicorn/http/message.py:26 defines VERSION_RE = re.compile(r\"HTTP/(\\d+)\\.(\\d+)\") with no ^/$/\\Z anchor and no bound on \\d+. parse_request_line (line 330) tokenizes with line_bytes.split(None, 2) at line 331, so bits[2] is the whole remainder of the request line and may contain spaces; the len(bits) != 3 guard at line 332 is structurally incapable of rejecting extra tokens because maxsplit=2 caps the list at 3. Line 352 then applies VERSION_RE.match(bits[2]) (prefix match, no fullmatch), so \"HTTP/1.1 arbitrary junk\" matches, line 353's `match is None` is False, and line 355 sets self.version = (1,1) while silently discarding the trailing attacker text. \"HTTP/0000000001.1\" / \"HTTP/9999999999.1\" are likewise accepted since \\d+ is unbounded. Reachability verified in real source, not just the graph: Request.parse at line 194 calls read_line (199/206, which enforces only the length limit and performs no token validation) then parse_request_line(line) at line 208; the graph agrees, with every call at lines 331/336/352/353/355 having reachable_from_entry=true and hop_distance=1. The resulting version tuple has genuine framing consequences: it drives keep-alive at message.py:165 (self.version <= (1, 0)), chunked-response framing at wsgi.py:284 (self.req.version <= (1, 0)), and is echoed verbatim into the response status line at wsgi.py:305-306, so an oversized version also forces chunked+keepalive and emits \"HTTP/9999999999.1 200 OK\" back toward the proxy. Version pinned to 21.2.0 (gunicorn/__init__.py:6), which predates the 22.0.0/23.0.0 request-parsing hardening; METH_RE at line 25 is still the old lax form, consistent with pre-fix code. Advisory exploit_search was unusable (corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), so no Metasploit module or fixed-version boundary is cited and no claim of a public weaponized exploit is made. Severity set to MEDIUM rather than the analyst's implied higher rating because gunicorn is documented as deployed behind a proxy and realizing smuggling requires a front-end that forwards or re-interprets the malformed request line differently; this is a lax-parsing/desync primitive, not a self-contained compromise. The analyst's claimed location (message.py:26-352, parse_request_line, CWE-444) is accurate."
