        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-444",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Components-with-known-vulnerabilities: the scanned component is gunicorn 21.2.0, pinned by the literal `version_info = (21, 2, 0)` at gunicorn/__init__.py:6 (and consumed as setup.py's `version=__version__`). Gunicorn 21.2.0 predates the 22.0.0 security release and is the affected range for the published HTTP request-smuggling CVEs in gunicorn's own parser (CVE-2024-1135, improper Transfer-Encoding/header validation; also tracked as CVE-2024-6827). The two unanchored-regex defects reported separately in this sweep (METH_RE, VERSION_RE at gunicorn/http/message.py:25-26) are first-party corroboration that this tree still carries the pre-22.x permissive parser literals. Recommend upgrading to a currently supported gunicorn release; a verifier should confirm the exact CVE-to-21.2.0 mapping against an advisory database rather than relying on this statement.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. (1) The version is pinned exactly at 21.2.0: `version_info = (21, 2, 0)` (gunicorn/__init__.py:6), consumed as `version=__version__` under `name='gunicorn'` (setup.py:87-88), and the in-tree changelog's newest entry is `21.2.0 - 2023-07-19` with no 22.x entry (docs/source/news.rst:5) \u2014 so this tree is genuinely older than the 22.0.0 security release. (2) Crucially, I confirmed the vulnerable pre-22.x request parser is actually PRESENT, not merely implied by a version string: in `Message.set_body_reader` (gunicorn/http/message.py:125-154) Transfer-Encoding is honored only on an exact `value.lower() == \"chunked\"` match, so any other TE value (`chunked, identity`, `xchunked`, quoted/parameterized) is SILENTLY IGNORED instead of rejected and execution falls through to the Content-Length branch; and there is NO Content-Length + Transfer-Encoding conflict rejection anywhere in the function. That pair is precisely the CL/TE desync primitive that gunicorn 22.0.0 fixed with strict TE validation, and it yields request smuggling against any front-end proxy that interprets the lenient TE value. Corroborating permissiveness in the same parser: unanchored `METH_RE` used with `.match()` (lines 25, 336) and unanchored `VERSION_RE` used with `.match()` (lines 26, 352) both accept trailing garbage in the request line, and obs-fold continuation lines are accepted unconditionally (lines 99-106). (3) Exploitability is strong: the graph reports `Message.parse_headers`, `Message.set_body_reader`, `Request.set_body_reader`, `Request.read_line` and `Request.proxy_protocol` all with `reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0` \u2014 this is the parse path for every inbound HTTP request to an internet-facing WSGI server. TWO CORRECTIONS to the analyst: (a) a `:Dependency` sweep returned only coverage, cryptography, eventlet, gevent, pytest, pytest-cov, sphinx, sphinx_rtd_theme \u2014 there is NO gunicorn dependency node, because gunicorn IS the scanned project rather than a third-party component it consumes, so the claimed CWE-1104 (\"Use of Unmaintained THIRD PARTY Components\") is the wrong class; the defect I verified in source is CWE-444 (inconsistent interpretation of HTTP requests). (b) `exploit_search` returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so I cite no Metasploit module and assert no weaponized public exploit; I likewise could not reach an advisory database, so I do NOT ratify the analyst's specific CVE-ID mapping (CVE-2024-1135 / CVE-2024-6827) \u2014 I confirm the underlying defect directly from source and the pre-22.0.0 boundary from the in-tree changelog, which stands independent of CVE numbering. The analyst's own caveat that a verifier should check the CVE mapping against an advisory DB remains unresolved here; the substantive finding does not depend on it."
