          "ruleId": "CWE-20",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "HEADER_RE = re.compile(r\"[\\x00-\\x1F\\x7F()<>@,;:\\[\\]={} \\t\\\\\\\"]\") at gunicorn/http/message.py:24 is a DENYLIST (used as `if HEADER_RE.search(name): raise InvalidHeaderName(name)` at line 93) rather than an RFC-7230 token allowlist, and its character set stops at \\x7F. Request bytes reach this check through `bytes_to_str` = `str(b,'latin1')` (gunicorn/util.py:650), which maps raw bytes 0x80-0xFF 1:1 onto codepoints U+0080-U+00FF -- none of which appear in the denylist. A request header name containing any high-bit byte (e.g. `X-Foo\\xA0Bar: v`, or a name made entirely of 0x80-0xFF bytes) therefore passes validation and is admitted into self.headers, while RFC 7230 forbids those octets in a field-name. The literal also omits `/`, `?` and `'`, which are likewise non-token characters. Divergent header-name acceptance between gunicorn and a front-end proxy is the precondition for header/request smuggling. parse_headers is an :EntryPoint handler (hop_distance 0). The same HEADER_RE denylist is reused on the response path at gunicorn/http/wsgi.py:252 (Response.process_headers, centrality 7.2e-5 -- a chokepoint every response flows through).\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. gunicorn/http/message.py:24 defines HEADER_RE as a DENYLIST character class whose highest codepoint is \\x7F, and it is the sole header-name check (message.py:93, `if HEADER_RE.search(name): raise InvalidHeaderName`). RFC 7230 specifies field-name as an allowlist (token = 1*tchar); this is its incomplete inverse. Raw request bytes reach the check via bytes_to_str = str(b,'latin1') (gunicorn/util.py:650, applied at message.py:67), mapping octets 0x80-0xFF onto U+0080-U+00FF -- none of which appear in the class -- so a header name containing any high-bit octet is admitted into self.headers. I independently found a concrete exploitation amplifier the analyst omitted: line 96 runs `name.strip()` AFTER validation at line 93, and Python's argless str.strip() removes Unicode whitespace, which in the Latin-1 range includes U+0085 (NEL) and U+00A0 (NBSP) -- both >\\x7F and thus invisible to HEADER_RE. Therefore `Transfer-Encoding\\xa0: chunked` decodes to \"Transfer-Encoding\\xa0\", uppercases (l.92), passes HEADER_RE (l.93), is then stripped to exactly \"TRANSFER-ENCODING\" (l.96), and matches set_body_reader at message.py:134, selecting the chunked reader in preference to Content-Length (l.138) -- a CL.TE desync primitive. The same validate-then-normalize window also reaches CONTENT-LENGTH (message.py:130), HOST/EXPECT/CONNECTION (wsgi.py:121-139, message.py:158) and the HTTP_ environ key (wsgi.py:136). I verified there is no other header-name validation layer between parse_headers and those consumers. Reachability is confirmed both ways: the graph reports the l.93 call (uid 8ea213bff4d02a1f568b1ddb990e26ee4f13b762) reachable_from_entry=true and Message.parse_headers as an :EntryPoint {kind:\"handler\", exposure:\"reachable\", hop_distance:0}, and the source path Request.parse -> parse_headers(data[:idx]) at message.py:233 confirms it is on the raw-socket request path. Version pinned at gunicorn/__init__.py:6 -> (21,2,0), i.e. before 22.0.0 where upstream replaced this denylist with an RFC-7230 token allowlist as a security fix. Two analyst errors corrected: (1) `'` IS a valid RFC 7230 tchar, so listing it as a missing non-token char is wrong (`/` and `?` are correctly flagged); (2) the response-path reuse at wsgi.py:252 is NOT a real issue -- send_headers latin-1 encodes (wsgi.py:322) and CR/LF are inside \\x00-\\x1F, so no response splitting is possible; I dropped it. Residual caveat on impact only, not on the defect: whether a given front-end forwards a `Transfer-Encoding\\xa0` name verbatim rather than dropping/rejecting it is deployment-dependent, and mcp__orion__exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so I cite no module/CVE version range."
